#{[:db/ident :activity.type/assignment {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045461 :db/ident :activity.type/assignment}] [:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank}] [:db/ident :answer-field.type/free {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408167 :db/ident :answer-field.type/free}] [:db/ident :answer.type/math {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math}] [:db/ident :answer.type/text {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text}] [:db/ident :course-academy.source/editor {:db/doc "User-directed content formatting edits in Course Academy." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502323 :db/ident :course-academy.source/editor}] [:course/id #uuid "c37c596e-77c4-5554-8295-443fb5da076b" {:course/code "MTH 255" :course/description "Vector Calculus II develops the calculus of vector fields and integration along curves and over surfaces. Building on Vector Calculus I, the course connects local descriptions of a field with accumulated quantities such as work, circulation, and flux. Its central tools include conservative fields, divergence, curl, and the integral theorems of Gauss and Stokes, with applications involving force, work, mass, and charge." :course/id #uuid "c37c596e-77c4-5554-8295-443fb5da076b" :course/outcomes [{:course-outcome/category "Differential Operators and Integral Theorems" :course-outcome/id #uuid "8b26130c-3ade-59f1-b5cd-30c4e77a4ee2" :course-outcome/index 9 :course-outcome/text "Explain and apply the Divergence Theorem to connect flux through a closed surface with a volume integral." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406505} {:course-outcome/category "Differential Operators and Integral Theorems" :course-outcome/id #uuid "11744f40-4941-5b05-bf74-21cff6dfbc86" :course-outcome/index 10 :course-outcome/text "Explain and apply Stokes’ Theorem to connect circulation around a boundary curve with a surface integral of curl." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406506} {:course-outcome/category "Differential Operators and Integral Theorems" :course-outcome/id #uuid "68ca80d7-e11e-58c2-9696-309503e57498" :course-outcome/index 8 :course-outcome/text "Calculate divergence and curl in standard coordinate systems and interpret them in relation to flux and circulation." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406507} {:course-outcome/category "Line Integrals and Conservative Fields" :course-outcome/id #uuid "9cd133e0-c54b-57f1-b597-4c2164a5bb07" :course-outcome/index 5 :course-outcome/text "Analyze conservative fields, find potential functions when they exist, and use path independence to evaluate line integrals." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406508} {:course-outcome/category "Line Integrals and Conservative Fields" :course-outcome/id #uuid "da10e544-4158-5cc3-9189-961bf8e19bf4" :course-outcome/index 4 :course-outcome/text "Evaluate scalar and vector line integrals and interpret their use in calculating quantities such as mass, work, and circulation." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406509} {:course-outcome/category "Surface Integrals and Flux" :course-outcome/id #uuid "c94983bd-7208-5af7-ab33-1ac7d94075a5" :course-outcome/index 6 :course-outcome/text "Set up and evaluate scalar surface integrals for quantities distributed over a surface, including mass or charge." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406510} {:course-outcome/category "Surface Integrals and Flux" :course-outcome/id #uuid "25423c4b-02b3-5fec-9546-7129ca1e1f95" :course-outcome/index 7 :course-outcome/text "Calculate flux through an oriented surface and interpret the role of the field’s normal component." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406511} {:course-outcome/category "Vector Geometry and Field Interpretation" :course-outcome/id #uuid "b93f8b7e-2e08-5fbc-b3b3-5fd74c01da7c" :course-outcome/index 2 :course-outcome/text "Explain the relationship among gradients, directional derivatives, and level sets." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406512} {:course-outcome/category "Vector Geometry and Field Interpretation" :course-outcome/id #uuid "3561ac1e-b8dd-59c8-b44a-28a8ad8f9fdc" :course-outcome/index 1 :course-outcome/text "Express vectors in standard coordinate systems and bases and interpret dot and cross products geometrically." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406513} {:course-outcome/category "Vector Geometry and Field Interpretation" :course-outcome/id #uuid "6ad6c8c5-38f2-5456-9c16-b54686cf0083" :course-outcome/index 3 :course-outcome/text "Construct line and surface elements appropriate to a curve or surface, accounting for its geometry and orientation." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406514}] :course/overview "The course revisits vector functions, space curves, gradients, and directional derivatives before extending integration to curves and surfaces. Line integrals connect vector fields with work and circulation. Conservative fields, potential functions, and path independence explain when an integral can be determined from endpoint information rather than the details of a path.\n\nSurface geometry and oriented surface elements provide the foundation for surface integrals and flux. Divergence and curl describe local field behavior, while the Divergence Theorem and Stokes’ Theorem connect these local quantities to integrals over regions and their boundaries. The archived course materials emphasize geometric understanding and the use of vector differentials to connect the various integration methods." :course/school "Oregon State University" :course/title "Vector Calculus II" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405204}] [:course/id #uuid "3ace2150-672f-50e7-9c2c-027b6d4272d7" {:course/code "MTH 341" :course/description "Linear Algebra I introduces the computational and conceptual study of linear systems, matrices, and subspaces. The course connects methods for solving equations with the structure of linear transformations and vector spaces. Determinants, linear independence, bases, dimension, and introductory eigenvalue analysis provide complementary ways to understand how a linear system or transformation behaves." :course/id #uuid "3ace2150-672f-50e7-9c2c-027b6d4272d7" :course/outcomes [{:course-outcome/category "Eigenvalues, Eigenvectors, and Introductory Applications" :course-outcome/id #uuid "c21e5c95-26cd-5756-9092-0f79c13dc069" :course-outcome/index 12 :course-outcome/text "Explain, at an introductory level, how eigenvalue methods can be used in solving simple systems of linear differential equations." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406537} {:course-outcome/category "Eigenvalues, Eigenvectors, and Introductory Applications" :course-outcome/id #uuid "d4780100-cd7f-58a2-8145-face3fff2460" :course-outcome/index 11 :course-outcome/text "Relate algebraic and geometric multiplicities to the availability of an eigenvector basis and diagonalize a matrix when such a basis exists." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406538} {:course-outcome/category "Eigenvalues, Eigenvectors, and Introductory Applications" :course-outcome/id #uuid "6b02fb05-51ae-5983-9ed2-7e2763532ea8" :course-outcome/index 10 :course-outcome/text "Calculate eigenvalues and eigenvectors, including examples involving nonreal complex values, and determine the associated eigenspaces." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406539} {:course-outcome/category "Linear Systems and Matrix Computation" :course-outcome/id #uuid "15723e90-f0cc-5ee0-9f2e-05c5f5aed323" :course-outcome/index 1 :course-outcome/text "Use Gaussian elimination to solve linear systems and describe their complete solution sets." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406540} {:course-outcome/category "Linear Systems and Matrix Computation" :course-outcome/id #uuid "26f3092c-2231-5b66-80de-d4a832531472" :course-outcome/index 3 :course-outcome/text "Calculate determinants and use their properties to assess invertibility and the uniqueness of solutions to square linear systems." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406541} {:course-outcome/category "Linear Systems and Matrix Computation" :course-outcome/id #uuid "3ef6ac4e-6cad-594f-83a6-5f66d9769392" :course-outcome/index 2 :course-outcome/text "Perform matrix operations and determine whether a square matrix has an inverse, calculating it when one exists." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406542} {:course-outcome/category "Linear Transformations" :course-outcome/id #uuid "a152871e-0fe3-5370-8e14-1c769972f5c4" :course-outcome/index 9 :course-outcome/text "Interpret matrix transformations geometrically and relate composition of transformations to matrix multiplication." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406543} {:course-outcome/category "Linear Transformations" :course-outcome/id #uuid "0ab3c082-09e3-558c-8ee4-d04898b4949c" :course-outcome/index 8 :course-outcome/text "Construct and use the standard-basis matrix representation of a linear transformation." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406544} {:course-outcome/category "Subspaces, Bases, and Dimension" :course-outcome/id #uuid "8c76e2f7-3c8c-5569-b169-2cc27e8fa04d" :course-outcome/index 7 :course-outcome/text "Apply the rank–nullity relationship to reason about solution sets and matrix invertibility." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406545} {:course-outcome/category "Subspaces, Bases, and Dimension" :course-outcome/id #uuid "d28777c9-3775-500a-8e78-8f1ec68fe02a" :course-outcome/index 5 :course-outcome/text "Analyze linear dependence and independence and determine the span of a collection of vectors." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406546} {:course-outcome/category "Subspaces, Bases, and Dimension" :course-outcome/id #uuid "35e7d779-26db-56d0-9c90-8d096e4d6754" :course-outcome/index 6 :course-outcome/text "Find bases and dimensions for row, column, and null spaces." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406547} {:course-outcome/category "Subspaces, Bases, and Dimension" :course-outcome/id #uuid "753ae7d6-d7b2-545e-b987-f70e367561c7" :course-outcome/index 4 :course-outcome/text "Determine whether a subset of \\(\\mathbb{R}^n\\) satisfies the requirements for a subspace." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406548}] :course/overview "Work with linear systems and Gaussian elimination establishes a foundation for matrix operations, invertibility, and determinants. The course then connects these calculations with linear independence, spanning sets, subspaces, bases, and dimension. Row, column, and null spaces describe important features of a matrix, while rank–nullity relates those spaces to the structure of a system’s solutions.\n\nMatrix representations make linear transformations concrete through examples such as rotation, reflection, projection, and scaling. Eigenvalues and eigenvectors introduce directions preserved by a transformation, including examples with complex eigenvalues. The archived section used here also introduces diagonalization and illustrates eigenvalue methods through simple systems of differential equations." :course/school "Oregon State University" :course/title "Linear Algebra I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405206}] [:topic/math-academy-id 57 {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049470 :topic/difficulty 1.147700024049995 :topic/encompasses [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429503 :encompassing/rationale "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account foundations: 1 rises / 3 eligible passed source activities = 0.333333; nearest selected fraction = 1/3. Crossing activity numbers [1]; waits [1]. Separate account estimates: foundations=1/3 (1/3). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: True. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/foundations-frequency-estimates.json, pair 57->1114. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json." :encompassing/topic {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048749} :encompassing/weight 0.3333333333333333} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429504 :encompassing/rationale "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Sel